## III. The solid

### polymorphs
- A polymorph is the same molecule in a different crystal packing. Ritonavir (Abbott, Norvir) was developed
  in late 1992, approved by the FDA on 1 March 1996 and marketed as a semisolid capsule and an oral liquid,
  with only one crystal form known through development and the NDA filing; attempts to find others failed.
  About two years after launch, in mid-1998, lots failed a dissolution specification, and investigation
  found a second form, designated Form II, more stable and much less soluble, precipitating out of the
  formulated product. Form II was not bioequivalent in the marketed dosage forms. Abbott announced
  difficulty manufacturing the capsules on 27 July 1998, the product was withdrawn and reformulated, FDA
  approved the reformulated capsule on 30 June 1999, and the European authorization followed on 1 December
  1999. The loss is estimated at more than US$250 million. Form III was found 24 years after Form II, and in
  2024 Sacchi and colleagues reported in *PNAS* that ball milling under selected solvent-assisted conditions
  could deliberately produce Form II and recover Form I.
- *Claude's gloss:* the molecular graph is identical across the forms and therefore irrelevant to the
  distinction that pulled a life-saving drug from shelves during the early HAART years. The distinguishing
  data is the symmetry and packing, which a registration system keyed on constitution cannot represent, and
  a patient relying on the drug bore the consequence.
**links:** crystal-symmetry · the-three-identities · computed-quantities.

### space-group-assignment
- Assigning the space group is the identity decision of a crystal structure, and the field has been counting
  its own errors for forty-five years. Marsh and Schomaker, "Some Incorrect Space Groups in Volume 16 of
  *Inorganic Chemistry*" (1979), began a series of such lists, and being named in one came to be called
  "being Marshed". Marsh's 1999 survey of every structure in the October 1998 release of the Cambridge
  Structural Database assigned to space group P1 found that of about 1,300 such entries, 279 had
  inappropriate designations — and in 157 of those, the reason was that the overline was missing from the
  symbol for P1̄, "probably because of difficulties in producing the symbol on word processors". A later
  survey found 115 more structures improperly described in P1. Marsh and Clemente's survey of the *Journal
  of the American Chemical Society*, from its first detailed crystal structure in 1940 through August 2006,
  found 188 cases described in space groups of inappropriately low symmetry, 135 of them previously
  unnoted.
- What the error does to the chemistry: "A feature of many of these structures is that, in the original
  space groups, bond lengths and angles are severely distorted; after revision of the space groups, the
  molecular geometries become normal."
- Spek's checkCIF, implemented in PLATON and first reported in 2003, validates submitted structures
  automatically, and its first class of tests — the 1xx series — addresses unit-cell and space-group
  symmetry. Spek's own 2018 question is "Forty years of Marshing: is the missed symmetry problem now
  solved?", and he notes that disorder, twinning and pseudo-symmetry blur the assignment, and that failing
  to report the best symmetry description "may lead to false claims of supposedly interesting chemistry that
  in hindsight are purely based on refinement artefacts".
- Raymond and Girolami, "Pathological crystal structures" (*Acta Cryst.* C79, 2023), catalogue the classes:
  misassigned atoms, high-symmetry superstructures containing guest molecules, incorrect space group,
  incorrect unit-cell size. Their central warning is about the fit statistic: "incorrect crystal structures
  often have good R values (least-squares residuals) and that these values are of little or no use in
  deciding whether a structure is correct". Fanwick's accompanying commentary is titled "Mythical
  compounds".
- *Claude's gloss:* 157 structures were wrong because a diacritic did not survive a word processor, which is
  the sharpest available demonstration that an identity carried as a string is an identity at risk from
  typography. The geometry result is the converse and the stronger one: the symmetry data is what makes the
  chemistry come out right, so a representation that keeps it is not tidier but more correct. And the R value
  is an observation whose fiber contains wrong structures — a fit statistic that does not warrant
  correctness, stated in the crystallographers' own words rather than derived.
**links:** crystal-symmetry · polymorphs · automorphism-is-physical · structure-elucidation.

### crystal-symmetry
- Crystallography never took the set quotient. A crystal structure is stored as a quotient presentation —
  unit cell plus space group generates the lattice — Wyckoff positions are the orbits of the space-group
  action on the cell, and site symmetry is computed as a matter of course. The 230 three-dimensional
  crystallographic space groups are a finite classification of exactly the structured-equivalence data of
  §4.2.
- *Claude's gloss:* this is the direction to build first. The symmetry data is already in the experimental
  record rather than something to reconstruct, the Cambridge Structural Database holds over a million
  determined structures, polymorphism has a documented eight-figure cost, and the identity half of the
  construction needs no collaboration with anyone. Measurement-side semantics — what an assay reading
  warrants — is the slower half and it is the argument for taking the solid before the potency.
**links:** polymorphs · automorphism-is-physical · molecular-graph · computed-quantities.

